1
00:00:05,303 --> 00:00:12,107
Hi, I'm going to walk you through OpenNavigator, a weather-aware truck routing app built for this full-stack assessment.

2
00:00:12,107 --> 00:00:16,265
A dispatcher enters an origin, destination, departure time, and load weight.

3
00:00:16,265 --> 00:00:26,472
OpenNavigator builds three real road routes, samples the forecast at the truck's estimated arrival time, applies the provided weather and load rules, and recommends the safest option.

4
00:00:26,472 --> 00:00:34,410
It is live at opennavigator dot two oh six dot eighty one dot six dot ninety seven dot sslip dot io.

5
00:00:34,410 --> 00:00:41,970
The goal is not just the fastest route; it is the route with the least weather exposure for the load.

6
00:00:42,771 --> 00:00:51,528
The stack is React and TypeScript on the front end, Django and Django REST Framework on the back end, and Postgres in production.

7
00:00:51,528 --> 00:00:58,000
Routing comes from O S R M, geocoding from OpenStreetMap Nominatim, and hourly weather from Open Meteo.

8
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,046
There are no paid A P I keys.

9
00:01:01,046 --> 00:01:12,848
The interface is built for dispatch: a planner, three route cards with risk summaries, an interactive Leaflet map with risk-colored route segments and checkpoint markers, and a forty-eight hour corridor heatmap.

10
00:01:12,848 --> 00:01:18,178
The map and heatmap are the primary view; the sidebar is the dispatch workflow.

11
00:01:18,981 --> 00:01:22,652
Let's plan a trip from New York to Boston.

12
00:01:22,652 --> 00:01:30,402
I'll keep the next available departure, set the load to forty-two thousand pounds, and sample weather every twenty-five miles.

13
00:01:30,402 --> 00:01:32,441
I'll click Plan safest route.

14
00:01:32,441 --> 00:01:45,902
The server geocodes both endpoints, requests alternatives from O S R M, and because O S R M sometimes returns fewer than three alternatives, the routing adapter adds via-point detours and deduplicates them.

15
00:01:45,902 --> 00:01:54,059
Then it builds checkpoints, groups them into six-hour weather buckets, and reads the forecast at each checkpoint's estimated arrival time.

16
00:01:54,059 --> 00:02:02,625
The app accepts a city, address, or latitude and longitude, and the departure window is limited to the fourteen-day forecast horizon.

17
00:02:03,427 --> 00:02:05,442
Here are the three routes.

18
00:02:05,442 --> 00:02:08,262
The recommended route is highlighted and explained.

19
00:02:08,262 --> 00:02:17,933
The ranking is safety-first: avoid No Travel when possible, then fewest severe miles, fewest high miles, lowest mileage-weighted average risk, and finally travel time.

20
00:02:17,933 --> 00:02:21,559
Route One is recommended and also the fastest here.

21
00:02:21,559 --> 00:02:26,394
The risk bar shows how the miles break down by risk level.

22
00:02:26,394 --> 00:02:33,244
Each card shows drive time, distance, arrival time, severe and high miles, and a zero-to-one-hundred risk score.

23
00:02:33,244 --> 00:02:40,497
The checkpoint log below shows every sampled point with its E T A, wind, rain, snow, and risk.

24
00:02:40,497 --> 00:02:44,526
Selecting another route updates the map and the checkpoint log.

25
00:02:44,526 --> 00:02:52,182
If any route has No Travel exposure, it is excluded from the recommended pool when a safer route exists.

26
00:02:52,986 --> 00:02:55,476
The map draws all three routes.

27
00:02:55,476 --> 00:03:00,041
The selected route gets a white casing and thicker risk-colored segments.

28
00:03:00,041 --> 00:03:02,531
Every checkpoint is a colored marker.

29
00:03:02,531 --> 00:03:05,851
I can zoom in and inspect the corridor.

30
00:03:05,851 --> 00:03:11,661
The selected route's checkpoint markers are larger; the other routes stay visible for comparison.

31
00:03:11,661 --> 00:03:18,302
The route geometry is sliced by checkpoint mileage, so the color changes where the risk changes.

32
00:03:18,302 --> 00:03:22,452
The checkpoint log is the same data in table form.

33
00:03:22,452 --> 00:03:29,092
Each row shows the mile, the estimated arrival time, wind, rain, snow, and the risk classification.

34
00:03:29,092 --> 00:03:34,487
Hovering over a row reveals the reasons behind the classification, including load-specific overrides.

35
00:03:34,487 --> 00:03:40,712
Checkpoints are sampled every ten, twenty-five, or fifty miles, and the destination is always included.

36
00:03:41,515 --> 00:03:46,377
The corridor heatmap shows how conditions evolve over the next forty-eight hours.

37
00:03:46,377 --> 00:03:49,619
The slider is measured from the departure hour.

38
00:03:49,619 --> 00:03:56,507
Moving to twelve hours, twenty-four hours, and forty-eight hours recolors the corridor using the same risk scale.

39
00:03:56,507 --> 00:04:05,015
The first frame for a route builds a dataset server-side and caches forty-nine hourly frames, so dragging the slider stays responsive.

40
00:04:05,015 --> 00:04:11,093
The heatmap samples the selected route every ten miles, capped at sixteen points for performance.

41
00:04:11,093 --> 00:04:14,740
I can also play the forecast as an animation.

42
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:21,628
The legend is the same Low, Moderate, High, Severe, and No Travel scale used by the checkpoints.

43
00:04:21,628 --> 00:04:26,895
The heatmap is intentionally per selected route so the three-corridor comparison stays readable.

44
00:04:27,698 --> 00:04:30,770
The risk model button opens the exact rules.

45
00:04:30,770 --> 00:04:34,993
Wind is classified in five-mile-per-hour bands from Low to No Travel.

46
00:04:34,993 --> 00:04:37,681
Rain and snow have separate inch-per-hour thresholds.

47
00:04:37,681 --> 00:04:41,136
Boundary values roll up to the higher risk category.

48
00:04:41,136 --> 00:04:57,646
Load rules are applied after the base weather risk: wind at or above fifty-five is No Travel for any load; forty-five to fifty-four with more than thirty thousand pounds is No Travel; thirty-five to forty-four with more than forty thousand pounds is Severe.

49
00:04:57,646 --> 00:05:01,870
The recommendation order and data sources are documented here as well.

50
00:05:01,870 --> 00:05:09,549
The model is conservative at boundaries: for example, rain at exactly zero point two five inches per hour is High.

51
00:05:10,352 --> 00:05:14,302
Under the hood, the backend follows a clean architecture.

52
00:05:14,302 --> 00:05:20,885
The domain layer contains pure Python for risk classification, checkpoint interpolation, risk totals, and recommendation.

53
00:05:20,885 --> 00:05:25,274
It has no Django or H T T P imports.

54
00:05:25,274 --> 00:05:33,175
The application layer orchestrates the use case through protocols: geocoder, routing provider, weather provider, repository, cache, and clock.

55
00:05:33,175 --> 00:05:41,952
The infrastructure layer implements those ports with Nominatim, O S R M, Open Meteo, and the Django O R M.

56
00:05:41,952 --> 00:05:48,097
The interface layer validates requests, maps errors, and serializes camel-case J S O N.

57
00:05:48,097 --> 00:05:55,558
That separation is what makes the risk rules easy to test at the boundaries and load overrides.

58
00:05:55,558 --> 00:06:00,386
The adapters are replaceable; the domain tests never touch the network.

59
00:06:01,187 --> 00:06:06,653
The test suite has ninety-seven backend tests and twenty-one frontend tests.

60
00:06:06,653 --> 00:06:18,082
Backend tests cover risk thresholds, load overrides, checkpoint interpolation, E T A selection, recommendation ordering, provider parsing, persistence, and A P I contracts.

61
00:06:18,082 --> 00:06:25,039
Frontend tests cover formatting, geometry slicing, the planner form, route cards, and heatmap controls.

62
00:06:25,039 --> 00:06:31,996
C I runs lint, tests, TypeScript checking, and the production build on every push.

63
00:06:31,996 --> 00:06:38,456
The repository includes architecture, A P I, risk model, deployment, and walkthrough documentation.

64
00:06:38,456 --> 00:06:46,904
The CI workflow runs on every push, and the build artifact is what gets rsynced to production.

65
00:06:47,706 --> 00:06:58,186
The app is deployed to the shared server with a dedicated system user, Postgres database, Gunicorn system D service, engine x, and Let's Encrypt T L S.

66
00:06:58,186 --> 00:07:06,725
Frontend builds happen locally because the server has about one gigabyte of RAM; only the static build and Python source are shipped.

67
00:07:06,725 --> 00:07:11,383
A daily system D timer backs up the database and runtime data.

68
00:07:11,383 --> 00:07:21,475
OpenNavigator prioritizes correctness and clarity: the risk model is isolated and tested, the recommendation is explainable, and the map makes weather exposure visible along the route.

69
00:07:21,475 --> 00:07:22,639
Thanks for watching.

70
00:07:22,639 --> 00:07:26,909
The project is live and the source is public on GitHub.
